@startuml one-turn
' One turn of the agent loop, end to end.
'
' The model proposes a call of a tool; the registry checks the call; the
' policy decides whether the call may run; the person answers when the rules
' ask; the scheduler runs what may run; the journal keeps what happened; the
' result goes back to the model and the turn goes on.
'
' The model proposes and does not decide: the registry and the policy decide
' what may run, and the journal is the only place where the history lives.
'
' Every block that asserts something about the code names the files that
' implement it, as `file:line'.  The names of the tools are the names the
' model calls them by.

start
:one turn of the agent
(the model is asked, the tools are dispatched)
lib/yeah-turn.el:1471;
:the model proposes a tool call
(read, edit, bash, git_status, todo_list)
lib/yeah-turn.el:1097;
:the registry checks the call
(type, name, arguments)
lib/yeah-tools.el:435;
:the policy decides
(may this call run?)
lib/yeah-perm.el:528;
if (the rules ask the person) then (yes)
  :the request goes to the person\nthe human answers: :once, :session, :project, :global or :reject\nlib/yeah-approval.el:59;
  if (the answer allows) then (yes)
    :the call may run;
  else (no)
    :the call is refused
    (the refusal is the result of the call)
    lib/yeah-turn.el:474;
  endif
else (no)
  if (the rules allow it) then (yes)
    :the call may run;
  else (no)
    :the call is refused
    (the refusal is the result of the call)
    lib/yeah-turn.el:474;
  endif
endif
:the scheduler runs what may run
(once-only, bounded, shared/exclusive)
lib/yeah-scheduler.el:294;
:the journal keeps what happened
(the only place the history lives)
lib/yeah-session.el:336;
:the result goes back to the model
(the turn goes on);
stop
@enduml
